		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Satellite pass times off by 1 hour...]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5027&amp;p=36562#p36562"><![CDATA[
Since Feb.... I've spent some time in and out of the hospital. This is the first time <br>since  the change to Daylight Savings Time in the USA that I've played around with<br>my TYT MD-380UV radio. <br><br>I went into the radio and changed the clock by 1 hour. I ran the Opengd77 CPS<br>and updated the satellite info. Now... when I go to the "Satellite" menu and it<br>predicts the satellites... but it seems like all of the predicted passes are off by<br>1 hour compared to the Gpredict <br><br>The radio is saying the next ISS pass starts at 15:37 (3:37pm)... by my Gpredict<br>says the next pass is at 16:37 (4:37pm). The radio is saying that the next PO-101<br>pass starts at 16:11 (4:11pnm)... but my Gpredict says the next pass is at 17:11<br>(5.11pm). I know my Gpredict is right as I heard an ISS pass on another radio<br>when Gpredict indicated it was in range.<br><br>When I first got this radio and put the OpenGD77 software into it... the radio and my<br>Gpredict software showed the satellite passes taking place at the same time. Like I<br>said... this is my first time playing around with the radio since we set the clocks<br>ahead 1 hour here in the USA.<br><br>Any suggestion as to what to look at so the radio displays the correct satellite pass<br>times???<br><br>I also seem to be having the same problem with my AnyTone HT (they recently<br>added satellite tracking to that radio in a recent firmware update). Need to<br>figure out why that radio is also off by one hour<br><br>Thanks,<br><br>Mike - N1EVH<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6514">n1evh</a> — Tue Apr 01, 2025 7:48 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
